  "query_preview": "..(Sunday through Friday)\nIssued at 233 PM EST FRI NOV 27 2026\n\nThe start of this period will be characterized by a strong upper \nlevel trough moving into the forecast area from the central US. This \ntrough will be the driver for our next big weather maker here in \neastern KY. Model guidance suggests that as this trough transverses \nfurther into the region, it'll dig and take on a slightly negative \ntilt signaling a strong/mature ML Cyclone. The attached surface low \nand cold front will bring high PoPs for Sunday into Sunday night and \nanother quick shot of cold air into the first part of next week. As \nmentioned, this system on Sunday will be quite strong dynamically \nbringing tremendous synoptic scale ascent and a screaming jet \nthroughout the column. The 500mb jet will likely max out at just \nover 100kts and the 850mb jet, more notably, will be greater than \n60kts. Instability and moisture looks to be very meager in the \nmodels right now, however. Dewpoints will likely stay in the low-50s \nwhich will restrict how unstable the atmosphere can be. Global \nmodels do suggest 50-100 j/kg of MUCAPE could be present, but even \nat this time of year that will likely not be enough for widespread \nthunderstorms. Moisture/instability should be watched in case they \ntrend upwards a bit as the system gets in range of the higher-res \nCAMs. Confidence in this occurrence is low, but it does happen from \ntime to time. What appears most likely at this time will be for a \nfast, strongly forced line of showers (with perhaps areas of \nembedded thunder)to move through the CWA sometime Sunday and/or into \nSunday night. The 32 degree temperature line will be over 500 miles \nto our north so we should be looking at an all rain event. Despite \nthe lack of meaningful instability, the wind field should be strong \nenough to bring very gusty winds down to the surface throughout the \nday and maybe even some pockets of damaging wind gusts with those \nshowers. After the cold front passes sometime Sunday night, winds \nand rain should gradually die down as we go into Monday. \n\nNext week will start out on the cooler side with highs in the 40s, \nbut highs should get warmer each day and we will likely see the \nreturn of the 60s by midweek. Ensembles do show a subtle shortwave \nmoving through the forecast area around the middle of next week but \nit is questionable at this time whether we squeeze any rain out of \nit or not. The next notable rain chances will come as we go into \nlate next week with another longwave trough and upper level low. So \nall in all we have a bit of an active pattern shaping over the next \nweek or so with at least 2-3 chances for measurable precip.",
